# `operators::geometry::levi_civita`

The Levi-Civita connection (Christoffel symbols) of a metric, as a function of the coordinates.

## Signature

```cpp
math::Function operators::geometry::levi_civita(const math::Function &metric, pluss::StackArray<std::string_view> coordinates = {}, operators::DiffOptions options = {});
```

## What It Does

A *metric* `g_ij(x)` says how to measure lengths and angles at each point of a space with coordinates `x¹…xⁿ`. The *Levi-Civita connection* is the rule, derived from the metric alone, for comparing vectors at neighbouring points without twisting them: its components are the Christoffel symbols

`Γⁱ_jk = ½ gⁱˡ (∂_j g_lk + ∂_k g_lj − ∂_l g_jk)`.

`levi_civita(metric)` computes them and returns a `Function` of the coordinates with output shape `[n, n, n]`, indexed `Γ[i][j][k] = Γⁱ_jk` (symmetric in `j` and `k`). It is a *function*, not an operator: build it once and pass it to [`covariant`](covariant.md) and [`absolute_differential`](absolute_differential.md).

The metric is a rank-2 `Function` (an `n × n` symmetric matrix) whose variables are the coordinates. If it has more variables than coordinates (a parameter, say) name the coordinates in `coordinates`, in order; the others stay as free variables of the connection. The metric must be invertible where the connection is evaluated: a singular metric is refused at evaluation.

## Parameters

`const math::Function &metric`
: The metric: a rank-2 `Function` of the coordinates with square output `[n, n]`.

`pluss::StackArray<std::string_view> coordinates`
: The coordinate variables in order, when the metric has other variables too. Empty (the default) means all of the metric's variables, one per dimension.

`operators::DiffOptions options`
: Options for the metric's derivatives.

## Returns

A `Function` of the coordinates with output shape `[n, n, n]`, `Γ[i][j][k] = Γⁱ_jk`.

## Errors

- `the metric must be a square matrix Function`.
- `one coordinate per metric dimension is required` when the variables do not match the dimension.
- A singular metric at evaluation raises `inverse domain excludes singular matrices`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The unit sphere, `g = diag(1, sin²θ)`. The non-zero symbols are `Γ^θ_φφ = −sin θ cos θ` and `Γ^φ_θφ = Γ^φ_φθ = cot θ`. At `θ = 1` they are `−0.45465` and `0.64209`; they sit at flattened positions 3, 5 and 6.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace geometry = pluss::operators::geometry;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const math::Function metric = math::function({"theta", "phi"}, [](math::Function theta, math::Function) {
    const math::Function one = theta * 0.0 + 1.0, zero = theta * 0.0, s = theta.sin() * theta.sin();
    return math::stack({math::stack({one, zero}), math::stack({zero, s})});   // diag(1, sin^2 theta)
  });
  const math::Function gamma = operators::geometry::levi_civita(metric);
  const auto point = {pluss::make_series(1.0), pluss::make_series(0.3)};   // theta = 1, phi = 0.3

  out.writeln("shape:", gamma.shape());
  out.writeln("Gamma[i][j][k] flattened:", gamma(point).reshape({8}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("-sin cos =", -std::sin(1.0) * std::cos(1.0), "  cot =", 1.0 / std::tan(1.0));
}
```

### Example 2

Flat space in polar coordinates, `g = diag(1, r²)`: `Γ^r_θθ = −r` and `Γ^θ_rθ = 1/r`. The plane is flat, so these are not curvature; they are the price of using a rotating frame.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace geometry = pluss::operators::geometry;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const math::Function metric = math::function({"r", "t"}, [](math::Function r, math::Function) {
    const math::Function one = r * 0.0 + 1.0, zero = r * 0.0;
    return math::stack({math::stack({one, zero}), math::stack({zero, r * r})});
  });
  const math::Function gamma = operators::geometry::levi_civita(metric);
  out.writeln("at r = 2:", gamma({pluss::make_series(2.0), pluss::make_series(0.3)}).reshape({8}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("Gamma^r_tt = -r = -2 at position 3,  Gamma^t_rt = 1/r = 0.5 at positions 5 and 6");
}
```

### Example 3

A metric with a parameter. The coordinates are named; the parameter `a` stays a free variable. A constant conformal factor cancels in the Christoffel symbols, so the connection of `a·diag(1, r²)` is the polar one for every `a`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace geometry = pluss::operators::geometry;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const math::Function metric = math::function({"r", "t", "a"}, [](math::Function r, math::Function, math::Function a) {
    const math::Function zero = r * 0.0;
    return math::stack({math::stack({a, zero}), math::stack({zero, r * r * a})});
  });
  const math::Function gamma = operators::geometry::levi_civita(metric, {"r", "t"});
  out.writeln("variables of the connection:", gamma.arity(), " shape", gamma.shape());
  for (const double a : {1.0, 5.0}) {
    out.writeln("a =", a, ":", gamma({pluss::make_series(2.0), pluss::make_series(0.3), pluss::make_series(a)}).reshape({8}).items<double>());
  }
}
```

### Example 4

The checks: one coordinate per metric dimension, a square metric, and an invertible one (the last is detected when the connection is evaluated).

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace geometry = pluss::operators::geometry;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto first_line = [](const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    return text.substr(0, text.find('\n'));
  };

  const math::Function three = math::function({"r", "t", "a"}, [](math::Function r, math::Function t, math::Function a) {
    return math::stack({math::stack({a, r * 0.0}), math::stack({r * 0.0, a})});
  });
  try { (void)operators::geometry::levi_civita(three); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("too many variables: ", first_line(e)); }
  const math::Function column = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return math::stack({x, x, x}); });
  try { (void)operators::geometry::levi_civita(column); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("not a square matrix:", first_line(e)); }
  const math::Function singular = math::function({"x", "y"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y) {
    const math::Function zero = x * 0.0;
    return math::stack({math::stack({x, zero}), math::stack({zero, zero})});
  });
  const math::Function gamma = operators::geometry::levi_civita(singular);
  try { (void)gamma({pluss::make_series(1.0), pluss::make_series(1.0)}); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("singular metric:    ", first_line(e)); }
}
```

